we're here in Central Park which is one  of the world's great urban spaces open  squares had existed in cities for  millennia but they were primarily places  of Commerce not Sylvan refuges from the  strife of city life after all nature was  seen as something dangerous looming  menacingly beyond city walls it was only  in the 19th century when Romantic poets  like Keats and Wordsworth began to  penetrate a church  city is filled up with migrants from  farms who presumably missed a little bit  of grass and a few flowers the downsized  extreme density the fetid water the  unclean air the mountains of human  refuge made the idea of a Sylvan retreat  even more appealing by the mid 19th  century New York City's 1811 grit plan  gave little space for open parks only  the parade now Madison Square Park was  marked on the plan as being a clearly  visible urban park william cullen bryant  the editor of the New York Evening Post  took up the call for a larger Park here  in New York Bryant had been born in a  log cabin in Massachusetts but he came  to New York and enjoyed huge success as  the editor of the New York Evening Post  he was joined by Andrew Jackson downing  the founding father of landscape  architecture in America and together  they waged a campaign for a greener  cleaner New York their campaign brings  forth legislation in 1853 which sets  aside 700 acres for a park it also  establishes a Park Commission so much of  the great 19th century infrastructure in  New York was built by state appointed  Commission's not local government the  Croton Aqueduct estate Commission the  Erie Canal Estate Commission Central  Park Estate Commission and so the Park  Commission held a contest to cop with  the best plan for a park that would  enrich the lives of ordinary New Yorkers  it was won by two young men Calvert veau  and Frederick Law Olmsted beau had been  a partner in Downing's office Olmsted  had no experience in landscape  architecture he'd been a journalist  whose writings on the antebellum south  still contains some of the most vivid  descriptions of that slave economy  together they designed a green sward  masterpiece a People's Park that would  be open to all they eschewed the  marble arches that would bar the  entrance for the poor instead they have  simple easy entry ways that welcoming  anyone and they filled their park with  marvels like this pond which in the  summer is filled with boats that bob up  and down there are 36 bridges designed  by Calvert Bois himself throughout the  park there's a sheep's meadow where  sheep themselves gambled on the green  until 1934 there's a castle the  Belvedere that rises out of the mists  it's not even short on the attempts to  impress civic values it's filled with  statues of 19th century icons like Sir  Walter Scott and William Shakespeare of  course the park does create challenges  most importantly maintenance and  programming all of this green space when  all of this humanity can create a mess  the park can easily get rundown so over  the course of the late 19th and early  20th centuries when Tammany Hall had  little use for a park that abutted the  buildings that were occupied by their  wealthy political enemies the park  decayed it was only in 1933 when the  reforming Mayor Fiorello LaGuardia was  elected that the park acquired a new  life he appointed the redoubtable Robert  Moses who took the park firmly in hand  Moses is responsible for much of the  park as indeed he is responsible for  much of New York City's infrastructure  but he's particularly associated with  the zoo  Central Park had had animals since 1859  when private donors started menagerie  but it was really Moses who gave the zoo  its modern form a form that is delighted  New York's children for generations  I particularly treasured the Delacorte  clock tower that was only built in 1965  where the animals danced around to the  sound of the chimes the park again  suffered to decay in the 60s and 70s  when New York's crime got out of control  as a kid I always felt that this was a  place of danger at least apart from the  prime visiting hours on weekends when I  had to commute through the park during a  transit strike  I talked loudly to myself in the hopes  that apparent insanity would deter  would-be mothers the famed jogger case  is a particularly notorious example of  crime in Central Park  of course the park did become safe again  as the city became safe and now  thousands of joggers go through the park  enjoying its pleasures programming is  the other challenge this great urban  space calls for humanity to use it well  perhaps the most important entrepreneur  in that regard was Joseph Papp who in  the 1960s began his Shakespeare in the  Park which brought some of the world's  greatest actors right here to the park  to performances that were free I have  fond memories of watching Kevin Kline  and Henry v their 40 years ago this park  is an urban marvel and it is a tribute  both to the vision of VAW and Olmsted  and to the value of having green space  within the city it is also a tribute to  the generosity of New York's land fists  who have helped to improve and maintain  the park over the years there are  legitimate public policy questions about  Central Park he's it too large would it  be better if it were carved into more  smaller parks and spread more evenly  throughout the city but while you can  ask those questions there's no doubt  that this park is an absolute treasure  for New York and a model of what truly  beautiful urban space can be  you
 

